you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's program  the never-ending rainy season we report  from south sudan at the front line of  the climate crisis  how nuclear fusion could play a major  role in our clean energy future  and flying high around britain to tackle  the climate crisis  we speak to conservationist sasha dench  ahead of her world record challenge  [Music]  hello there and welcome to the uk's only  daily climate news show  where we track the changes that are  happening to our world  right now and meet those who are coming  up with the solutions  now south sudan is just 10 years old but  its early years have been difficult ones  a civil war only ended last year and  more than half the population  face acute food insecurity well now  climate change has become  the most pressing challenge the un says  1.6 million people  have been affected by flooding our  africa correspondent john sparks reports  from boar county  they say water is a blessing in south  sudan  but the people who live here have never  experienced  anything like this  vast areas lie underwater after two  years of unprecedented flooding  with crops and fields and homes for 200  000 covered by an inland sea  we're following the road north in the  direction of sudan  but there's very little left of the  highway some of the richest  agricultural land in the country now  used by a handful of fishermen  the majority who lived here in jungle a  state  have departed for higher ground but  there are a few stragglers left  trying to hang on the water is keeping  on increasing  it keeps increasing it keeps on  increasing this is only a problem we are  facing here  the flood waters have spread inland from  the river nile  and last year's rainy season never  stopped  the un is now trying to restore an  ageing dike  all of this area of south sudan has  changed on the map  and so they've lost vast stretches of  land and they've lost  areas of land where people were living  and farming before  many here say the flooding is part of  god's plan  but government officials say otherwise  it is when you have given up  of thinking scientifically that way you  will say is god  but to us we say it is a global warming  those who remain here eat water lilies  and fish  in a new world overwhelmed by water  john sparks sky news in south sudan  and you can see more from john's time in  broad county that's on our weekly  digital show  that will be available on sky news  social channels our app  and website from saturday morning now  for decades  nuclear fusion has been talked about as  the ultimate solution to the world's  energy needs  and by extension the climate crisis it's  a limitless source of clean energy  without any of the harmful byproducts  that produced by nuclear fission well  that's how our nuclear power is produced  today  well if it sounds too good to be true  that's because the processes involved  have always proved too complicated  for scientists to replicate at scale but  efforts continue and this week a company  backed by amazon founder jeff bezos  confirmed plans to build its first  nuclear fusion plant  here in the uk so what is nuclear fusion  all about we're here to explain more  is dr amy gandy senior lecturer in  nuclear materials engineering at the  university  of sheffield dr gandhi good to talk to  you could you just explain to us  how this works well the general concept  with fusion  is that we're trying to force really  small particles together  and when you can force these particles  together they actually fuse and when  they fuse they generate  new particles and these new particles  have an awful lot of energy associated  with them  and we can harness that energy to  generate electricity  what stops it from being scalable  well we can at the moment achieve fusion  so we can create  these fusion plasmas so this is where  we're heating our small particles  together  to ten times hotter than the sun so they  become a gas type  state called plasma and this allows them  to be affected by magnetic fields  and this means we can fuse these  particles together even though they're  really high temperatures so we can  actually achieve that even though it  sounds quite incredible  the challenge at the moment is the fact  that we have to put so much energy in  compared to how much energy we get out  so this is one of the main challenges  that all of these new reactors that are  in development that are going to be  built around the uk and around the world  are trying to solve so these are the  main challenges and if we overcome those  challenges how much of our energy do you  think it could create  well a fusion reaction if we're thinking  about it on mass by the amount of  fuel that we need can generate million  times more energy  than burning fossil fuels so it can  generate an awful lot of energy  and as you said at the beginning and it  won't produce the same levels of  radioactive waste that we have at the  moment for nuclear fission  the map the fuels that we use are  readily available so  um they are things like lithium and  things that we can get  from water so the isotopes of hydrogen  so deuterium and tritium  so they're readily available and and  obviously water is a never-ending supply  and also fusion is inherently safe  so um we can't have a runaway chain  reaction like you would with nuclear  fission the technology  is essentially completely the opposite  is a different type of physics  that's been fascinating talking to you  dr amy gandy thank you  now let's bring you some more of today's  climate news now  and it might seem strange given the  thunderstorms and the rain that we've  been seeing across the uk today  but weather warnings for extreme heat  have been launched after a  record-breaking number of  heatwave deaths were recorded in england  last summer  public health england recorded 2256  excess all-cause deaths during three  periods that are officially classed as  heat waves last summer  the highest number since its records  began in  2004.  there are worries that the un climate  summit in glasgow later this year is  headed for failure  following little progress in three weeks  of preparation discussions  one obstacle to progress is the failure  by wealthy countries  to deliver on their promise to deliver  climate finance to less developed  countries  there are also questions over whether  some country's delegates will be able to  attend cop  26 at all with many poorer nations  unable to access  vaccines after supplies were brought up  by  richer countries  now one for the fashionistas gucci has  developed a  vegan alternative to leather made with  77  plant-based materials it's named dimitra  in reference to the greek goddess of  harvest  the luxury brand's vegan leather  contains viscose and wood pulp compounds  that are sourced from sustainable  managed forests  now there are all sorts of ways to raise  awareness about climate change and one  person hoping to inspire change  is sasha dench sasha otherwise nicknamed  as the human swan  is taking on a challenge that's never  been done before  in a bid to break two guinness world  records she will attempt to fly around  the entire coast of britain  in a wind and green electric powered  paramotor  the conservationist will use the 3 000  mile journey to meet the people tackling  climate change  and prove solutions can be found through  new ideas  well the human swan sasha joins  me now uh really good to talk to you  sasha  looking at these images here it's quite  phenomenal to see you  up there in the sky in that contraption  what does it feel like to be flying with  the birds  well flying with birds is absolutely  amazing they definitely see you as  another bird in the air they quite often  will come and try and fly with you  particularly if you're firmling because  they'll look for other signs in the air  of um of where they might be updrafts so  that is incredible  but you are obviously um hanging from a  seat high up in the air so it can feel  also quite fragile  it sounds absolutely petrifying if you  ask me is it dangerous  um i've been doing it for quite a long  time the key thing is really that  the thing that flies is the the wing  part of it the motor part  just keeps you airborne um and yeah  the the nice thing is what i love about  it is that when you're in the air you  are quite like a bird because you're  very attached to the wing so  you can read exactly what the air is  doing and that's the most important  thing  and if it all goes really wrong you have  also got a spare parachute in your  pocket but  um yeah i've been doing it long enough  that um actually being up in the sky is  a happy place for me  oh well it looks tremendous and other  than wanting to break  a guinness world record you've got a  serious mission though haven't you about  climate change sasha  yeah so there's two guinness world  records one of them is me putting skin  in the game i do expeditions with  migratory birds in a normally in a  petrol-powered paramotor  and i want to see how far you can go  switching to to electric  um ditching the the fuel from there and  the second thing though  is trying to rally the whole country  around  a different idea of climate change no  longer having it as being this  dark cloud on the horizon a kind of  climate again trying to turn  into a big opportunity there are so many  great  stories great people great ideas around  the country  where people are taking the the climate  challenge head-on  and finding solutions for it and they're  from all sorts of industries they're  from you know big industry they're from  farming  they're people doing things locally and  that's what i want to spotlight i want  to see them for myself as well  i would like to to be inspired by that  and  really believe that britain drove the  industrial revolution and we really can  drive the green revolution too that's  the story i'm hoping to be able to tell  by stopping and meeting  to all these different people around the  country well sasha dench  wishing you a very safe flight we'll  keep our eyes  up to the sky perhaps we might see you  thank you thank you  now from one intrepid explorer to  another  sailor tobias carter's work takes him  all the way to the arctic  the eco adventurer has led teams on  several polar expeditions  to measure the impact of climate change  from the front line  he's hoping to raise greater awareness  of the threat facing the natural world  [Music]  i'm just a simple citizen concerned like  many about the future of our planet  discovering sailing and what's happening  to the world  i wanted to take action  [Music]  we're here to show people the impacts of  climate change  by the stories of the people the  scientists and the locals that  see these impacts first hand with our  vessel north about we also participate  in various research programs  to better anticipate and understand  climate change  we hope to convince people here in  europe that climate change is not in 50  or 100 years  is happening now  greenland is so important to the changes  affecting our climate  climate change isn't this far-fetched  idea people here  are seeing first-hand the changes  already  affecting our world  the one important thing about climate  change it's got to be  hope society is changing it doesn't  happen  overnight but it's already changing at  an incredible pace  and let's change the world together  all these wonderful adventures today  well that is it for today's show  but you can get your climate fixed still  over the weekend with our podcast on  this week's episode  we're talking to climate scientist dr  michael e mann  about his new book the new climate war  that's on climatecast  and it's available for you wherever you  normally get your podcasts from  thanks for watching hope to see you next  week

